What I've learned

The physics and the parts

The detector is a CsI(Tl) crystal turning a gamma into a flash of light, and a SiPM turning that flash into a voltage pulse. Pulse height equals gamma energy, and a histogram of pulse heights is the spectrum.
The same pairing is used by RadiaCode, so the concept works. Your uncertainty is the coupling: CsI(Tl) emits at ~540 nm, where the SiPM is less sensitive, and I don't know the exact margin. That decides your resolution.
Alpha is blocked by the wrap and beta mostly is too, so you get no particle-type discrimination.
The SiPM must never go above ~29.7 V. Its gain drifts ~0.8%/°C, so temperature is the main accuracy problem.

Design decisions

The OpenGammaKit BOM (MAX1422, transformer, digital pots) is a different design and shouldn't be mixed with OGD.
A peak-hold circuit decouples pulse shape from ADC sampling speed. That's what makes dropping the comparator workable.
Firmware thresholding removes parts and tuning but adds code risk. Keep the debug tap and test pads.
A single 2-layer board is enough at µs speeds, and it cuts both fab cost and routing difficulty.
Don't use a BAT54 on the boost output. The ADC reference, the NTC placement and the 50 V cap ratings are the details that bite if you miss them.

